# profiling — design notes

Design history for the `profiling` skill, and specifically for `npm run perf:ios:webkit:gates`
(`tools/perf/ios/capture-webkit-gates.mjs` + `tools/perf/lib/webkit-inspector.mjs`), whose shape is almost entirely
dictated by protocol facts that are expensive to rediscover.

## Driving a physical iPad: what is actually true

The runbook used to say Apple "exposes no CDP/automation socket for a physical device." That is
right about **CDP** and wrong about automation. Safari on a real device speaks the **WebKit
Inspector Protocol** over USB — the same channel Web Inspector uses — and it carries
`Runtime.evaluate` and `Console.messageAdded`, which is the entire gates loop. The correction is in
the runbook now; the claim as written had discouraged anyone from looking.

Verified on **iPadOS 26.5** (device `Kyle's iPad`), macOS Tahoe, July 2026.

### `ios-webkit-debug-proxy` works — the spike expected it wouldn't

Issue #636 predicted brew's `ios-webkit-debug-proxy` 1.9.2 was a dead end, reasoning that it speaks
the legacy usbmuxd/lockdown path Apple replaced with RemoteXPC in iOS 17. That reasoning is sound
and the conclusion is still wrong: the inspector relay works fine on iPadOS 26.5. It enumerated the
device, listed Safari's tabs, and relayed the full protocol. Whatever RemoteXPC changed, it did not
take `com.apple.webinspector` with it.

**`pymobiledevice3` was therefore never needed**, and neither was its `sudo`-for-the-tunnel problem
— the concern that most threatened putting this in an npm script. Do not reach for it unless the
proxy actually breaks on some future iPadOS.

### Five protocol shapes that dictate the client

These are why `lib/webkit-inspector.mjs` is not a thin wrapper over a CDP client:

1. **Everything multiplexes through the Target domain.** A bare `Runtime.evaluate` answers
   `'Runtime' domain was not found` — which reads like a missing capability and is really a missing
   envelope. Commands must be JSON-encoded inside `Target.sendMessageToTarget` and their replies
   pulled back out of `Target.dispatchMessageFromTarget`, on an id space separate from the outer
   one. The `Target.targetCreated` event is where the `targetId` comes from, so a client has to wait
   for it before it can send anything at all.
2. **There is no `awaitPromise`.** That is a CDP parameter; WebKit ignores it and hands back the
   promise object. This is *the* structural reason the runner fires the driver and then polls
   `window.__perfRows` instead of awaiting the injected IIFE. Any future "just await the payload"
   refactor will silently get an empty object.
3. **`returnByValue` does not return structured values.** Anything non-primitive comes back as an
   opaque remote object. Hence the `readJson()` convention — evaluate `JSON.stringify(expr)` and
   parse in Node. Cheap, total, and it sidesteps remote-object handles entirely.
4. **A suspended tab hangs instead of failing.** iOS suspends a backgrounded Safari tab; it still
   lists in the relay and still announces an inspector target, but never runs JS. So a command
   against it produces *no reply at all* rather than an error, and the first-listed tab is not
   reliably the foreground one. Tab choice is therefore a short-budget liveness probe, not
   `pages[0]`. This cost a real debugging session: every run during development happened to have
   exactly one tab open, so `pages[0]` was always right until it wasn't, and the symptom was a
   30-second hang on `Runtime.enable` with nothing in the protocol saying why.
5. **A tab announces two targets.** A `frame` target arrives alongside the `page` target, in no
   guaranteed order. Keeping whichever announced last addresses every subsequent command to a frame.
   Filter on `type === 'page'`.

### Why the tab is navigated on every run

Not convenience. The two failures that cost real measurements during the #446 verification were a
stale tab silently running an old bundle, and a leftover `window.__perfScenarios` quietly scoping a
"full" run to one scenario. Both are invisible in the output — the table looks completely normal. So
the runner navigates the tab itself rather than trusting one that already shows the harness URL, and
assigns *every* `window.__perf*` override on each run rather than only the requested ones. The
overrides are locked by `tools/perf/tests/ios-webkit-gates.test.mjs`; the navigation is not, because faking
the device is more machinery than the guarantee is worth.

### What stays manual, and why

* **Timeline recording.** The protocol has a `Timeline` domain, but its event stream is not the
  shape `npm run perf:analyze:web-inspector` parses (that reads a Web Inspector *export*,
  `{recording:{records,markers}}`). Automating the gates run does not get Timeline capture for free,
  and the issue scoped it out. A5–A6 of the runbook remain the only path. Anyone attempting it
  should expect to write a second analyzer, not to reuse the existing one.
* **Opening Safari with a tab.** The relay lists Safari's *existing* tabs; with none open there is
  nothing to attach to and no protocol call that creates one. So a human still unlocks the iPad and
  opens Safari once. Everything after that is automated.

## The real-screen probe (`perf:ios:webkit:frames`) — what the measurements cost to learn

Design history for `tools/perf/probes/real-screen-probe.js` + `lib/real-screen-stats.mjs` +
`ios/capture-webkit-frames.mjs` (issue #659). The shape of all three is dictated by wrong readings
that had to be corrected against real device captures, which is also why the probe **records raw
tables and computes nothing**: every metric definition below was fixed without re-drawing anything.

### Safari gives web content 60 Hz on a 120 Hz ProMotion iPad

Measured on iPad13,8 / iPadOS 26.5: a bare rAF sampler reports 16–17 ms **idle and animating**. The
first version of the stats module assumed the 8.33 ms ProMotion beat and duly reported a
perfectly-paced capture as **64% late frames**. Budgets are now derived per capture
(`observedFrameIntervalMs`, p10 of observed deltas).

Two consequences outlive the probe: ADR-0066's 8.3 ms commit-hitch gate is stricter than the platform
on Safari/iPad, and a 120 Hz+ digitizer feeding a 60 Hz frame means per-`pointermove` work runs 2–4×
per presentable frame.

### Four metrics that measured the operator instead of the app

Each looked like a finding first:

* **Move gaps** spanned stroke boundaries, so a human pausing between strokes read as a 4.5-second
  input gap. Now within-stroke only.
* **Paint latency** was per frame (frame → newest move), so a finger held still grew it without
  limit. Now per move, skipping each stroke's last move, because the idle after a finger-lift is not
  latency.
* **The stroke-end hitch** reported a page going idle with nothing to animate as a 2.4-second hitch.
  Now capped, with the implausible ones counted as `idleAfterLift`.
* **Stroke segmentation** windowed events per phase, which dropped the last stroke of *every* phase —
  a phase's clock runs while the finger is down, so it always ends mid-stroke. Strokes are now
  segmented over the whole recording and claimed by the phase their pointerdown fell in.

### A p95 over 20–40 samples is not a distribution

The costliest wrong turn. Two otherwise identical device runs disagreed about whether the pointer
halos cost ~100 ms at every finger-lift: run 1 showed a clean 104 ms-vs-17 ms split along
halos-visible/hidden, run 2 showed 17 ms almost everywhere with a ~120 ms outlier in *every*
configuration. The difference was sample size — 19 strokes in one phase, 27 in the next, so the p95
picked a different rank. Per-stroke figures are reported as **rates** now (`stalled lifts` out of
measured lifts). The halo claim was withdrawn.

### A hand-drawn A/B cannot attribute anything

A human ran 7 to 45 strokes per phase at 1.9–4.2 moves per frame. The suppression deltas came out
non-monotonic (hiding the halos looked *worse* than baseline) purely because that phase happened to
be the one with 41 short strokes. Hand-drawn runs establish that the lag exists; `--drive` establishes
what causes it. For a hand-drawn comparison, repeat phases (`page,page-no-halos,page,page-no-halos`)
so variability cancels — repeats are labelled `key#2` rather than shadowing each other — and read the
per-bucket table, since a phase can only fairly be compared to *itself earlier*.

### Two ways of driving synthetic input at a real rate, both wrong

The rate matters because a hand delivers 1.9–4.2 moves per presentable frame and one-per-frame
measured perfectly clean.

* `setTimeout(8.3)` delivers ~13 ms on device (nested-timer clamping), so a run labelled "120 Hz"
  actually ran at 83 Hz and its clean result meant nothing.
* A `MessageChannel` spin loop hits any rate and **starves the event loop** — it stopped the app's own
  coloring-page setup from settling, and starving the loop is precisely what was under measurement.
  An instrument that writes its own result is not an instrument.

Now: a timer at the frame rate dispatching however many moves the elapsed time earned, bounded so a
stall is never followed by a burst real coalescing would never produce.

### The finding, and the wall

Frame production stalls for 335–1422 ms while `pointermove` keeps being **handled on time** — 8.3 ms
apart, 5–7 ms queue delay, with 0–5 ms of `engine.*` inside the whole stall. So it is neither slow JS
nor lost input: the app is starving the rendering pipeline, which is exactly the reported "freezes,
then catches up in a jump".

**No synthetic input reproduces it.** Ruled out on device, all clean: 1 move/frame, 4.03 moves/frame
at `pointerType: 'pen'` on a coloring page, the on-device HUD as a confound, accumulated undo-history
rasters to 33.9 MB, and every CSS suppression of the blend nudge / `mix-blend-mode` / halos. What
remains is what `dispatchEvent` cannot fake — the real iOS touch pipeline, with **iPadOS Scribble** the
prime suspect (ADR-0038 and `scribbleGuard` exist because a stylus tap can arm it; recognition would
be main-thread work inside the browser, invisible to every instrument here, present only under a real
Pencil). The cheap decisive test is Settings → Apple Pencil → Scribble OFF followed by one hand-drawn
run.

### The one production seam, and why it is read-only

`lib/boot/devHarnessSeam.ts` exposes the already-exported `getUndoDebug()` on `/` behind the
`routes/dev/*` gate. It exposes no mutation deliberately: a probe that can change the app can
invalidate its own measurement. Everything else the probe needs it gets by driving the real UI by
selector (coloring page, brush), so there is no probe-only surface in production — and the selectors
are drift-guarded by a test, since prose cannot hold that agreement.

## Open / unvalidated

* Only ever run against one device (iPadOS 26.5). `--device-id=` exists for multi-device machines
  but has not been exercised with two attached.
* The gates thresholds are not enforced by `perf:ios:webkit:gates` — it prints the table and the gate text, like
  the manual paste. `perf:web:undo:webkit` is the one that exits non-zero past `COMMIT_GATE_MS`. Making
  the device run gate-enforcing would need a view on device-to-device variance nobody has yet.
* Every measured column on iPadOS 26.5 came back at or under 1 ms, against a 1 ms
  `performance.now()` clamp — the run proves the plumbing, but the device is currently so far inside
  the gates that the numbers can't discriminate. A regression hunt will want the Timeline, not this
  table.
